.title = "The Power of Soundtracks",
.date = .date("2026-08-03T14:17:00"),
.authors = ["medzernik"],
.layout = "post.shtml",
.draft = true,
---

It's no secret that I absolutely love game and to some extent even movie/TV show soundtracks. They bring emotions in such a way that no other music genre can. 

Games are intense experiences that are interactive. This means they can invoke emotions by having you perform various actions, in addition to the audio-visual experience and narrative that can be experienced in the movies. 

> If you want to learn more about games and their development, I heavily recommend the book [Designing Games](https://tynansylvester.com/book/) by Tynan Sylvester. Aside from being a seasoned game dev, he also touches on many psychological aspects of games and their cultural impact.

Having played ehm.. *more than enough* games, I can definitely say I experienced plenty of moments that were either happy, sad, annoying or extremely emotional (to the point of crying). All of these moments were accompanied by a soundtrack (except for some aftermaths that purposefully lacked any soundtrack altogether, which was also extermely powerul as a tool!). 


# The Genius of Mike Morasky

